# Why is water cheap and diamonds dear?
subtitle: "Adam Smith's puzzle: why is life-giving water cheap and useless diamonds dear? Answer, then see the last glass of water explain it."

## Why is water cheap and diamonds dear?
text: {"font_size":34}
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 1st
      - 2nd
      - 3rd
      - 4th
      - 5th
      - 6th
    values:
      - 100
      - 45
      - 22
      - 12
      - 6
      - 3
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 110
    y_label: Worth of one more glass
    x_label: Glasses of water in a day
    bar_colors: same
    box:
      x: 140
      'y': 230
      w: 780
      h: 400
sources:
    - id: smith
      title: An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations, Book I, ch. 4
      authors: Adam Smith
      year: 1776
      finding: nothing is more useful than water, but it will purchase scarce any thing; a diamond has scarce any value in use, but a very great quantity of other goods may be had in exchange for it

    icon water cites ["smith"] size 280 x 280 y 450 icon_look picture of droplet hidden true
    icon gem cites ["smith"] size 280 set twemoji x 720 y 450 icon_look picture of "gem stone" hidden true
    text cheap "cheap" #2f6b8f x 280 y 250 font_size 54 hidden true
    text dear "dear" #b5482e x 720 y 250 font_size 54 hidden true
    path price dashed thick #2f6b8f points [[140,266.3636363636364],[920,266.3636363636364]] hidden true
    text price_l "price of a glass" #2f6b8f x 800 y 306.3636363636364 font_size 32 hidden true
    text stop "stop here" #b5482e x 660 y 380 font_size 38 hidden true
    arrow stop_a #b5482e from.x 660 from.y 350 to.x 660 to.y 274.3636363636364 hidden true
    text mu "marginal utility" #2f6b8f x 470 y 470 font_size 42 hidden true

    card title "Why is water cheap and diamonds dear?"

A: In 1776 Adam Smith posed a puzzle. Nothing is more useful than water, yet it buys almost nothing.
    draw water cheap

B: And diamonds are no use at all, but cost a fortune. Is that a puzzle? Diamonds are pretty.
    draw gem dear

    quiz "Why is water cheap and diamonds dear?"
        right: Price follows the last glass, not the first: we have so much water that one more is worth very little.
        * Diamonds are more useful
        + There is so much water that one more glass is worth little
        * Diamond prices are set by law

    hide water gem cheap dear

B: Hang on. What do you mean, the last glass?
    draw axes bar_1

A: Picture your glasses of water in a day. The first one keeps you alive.
    highlight bar_1

A: The next ones go on tea, on washing, on the plants. Each is worth a little less than the one before.
    draw bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5 bar_6

B: So I keep going until one more glass isn't worth what it costs.
    draw price price_l

A: Exactly. And water is so plentiful that its price is tiny, so you go far out, to where one more glass is worth next to nothing.
    draw stop stop_a

A: Diamonds are so scarce that even the next one is still way up here.
    icon gem2 size 130 set twemoji x 830 y 560 icon_look picture of "gem stone"
    path gem2_line dashed #8f887c points [[140,560],[760,560]] style.stroke_width 2
    text gem2_l "scarce: still up here" #b5482e x 560 y 600 font_size 34

    erase gem2_l

A: Economists call it marginal utility. It took them a century after Smith to work it out, in the 1870s.
    draw mu

B: So in a desert, I'd swap my ring for a bottle.
    highlight gem2

A: Gladly. In the desert, the last glass is the first.
    highlight bar_1

